Scout Master'ship in seven minutes or less.

55
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:32,400
Just yesterday- this is Sunday- in a feature that we call Baden Powell's blog, where we're posting something that Sir Robert Baden Powell, the founder of the worldwide scouting movement, wrote during his lifetime,

56
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:37,400
And the title of this week's post was The Other Fellow's Point of View,

57
00:09:38,400 --> 00:09:43,400
And I want to quote from that a little bit to introduce the idea of an internal standard to you.

58
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:55,400
Baden Powell wrote: we in the Scouts desire not so much to cure present social evils as to prevent their reoccurrence in the rising generation.

59
00:09:56,400 --> 00:10:07,400
If our lads were trained as a regular habit to see The Other Fellow's Point of View before passing their own judgment on a dispute, what a difference it would make.

60
00:10:07,400 --> 00:10:19,400
So just this idea, this one aspect of character, of understanding The Other Fellow's Point of View, got me to thinking about what defines the character that we're trying to inspire.

61
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:34,400
What is it that we can do for our scouts so that they have the capacity, as Baden Powell said, they are trained as a regular habit to see The Other Fellow's Point of View before passing their own judgment.

62
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:42,400
And I think that the character we are trying to inspire is perfectly defined in the Scout Oath and Law.

63
00:10:43,400 --> 00:10:45,400
It doesn't really need any more embroidering than that.

64
00:10:46,400 --> 00:10:58,400
The Scout Oath and Law stand pretty much on their own as an expression of values and ideals that have been the common currency of every enlightened human society throughout history.

65
00:10:59,400 --> 00:11:01,400
How about that? I mean, that's a pretty big claim, isn't it?

66
00:11:02,400 --> 00:11:09,400
How do we affect our scouts? How do we accomplish the idea of getting them to internalize the Scout Oath and Law?

67
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:15,400
So it's something that, throughout the rest of their lives, that they can call on as they set their path in life.

68
00:11:16,400 --> 00:11:23,400
And I would say we do that by creating opportunities for our scouts to exercise their understanding of the Scout Oath and Law,

69
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:38,400
To build this internal standard of ideals and conduct that, as Baden Powell said, don't so much cure present social evils as prevent their recurrence in the rising generation.

70
00:11:39,400 --> 00:11:48,400
So what opportunities do we create? Well, just everything that scouts do, of course, will give them experiences that call upon that an internal standard:

71
00:11:48,400 --> 00:12:01,400
Working together as a patrol and going camping and meetings and doing everything that scouts do. they have to call continuously on that internal standard to be able to listen to the other fellow's point of view

72
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:08,400
and to make good judgments and good choices about the way that they're going to approach things and the way that they're going to behave.

73
00:12:09,400 --> 00:12:15,400
There are other really important opportunities that sometimes we may miss. One of them is Scoutmaster Conferences.

74
00:12:15,400 --> 00:12:23,400
In the advancement scheme that is followed by the Boy Scouts of America, prior to advancing in every rank you have to have had a Scoutmaster Conference,

75
00:12:24,400 --> 00:12:35,400
And there's a lot of verbiage about Scoutmaster Conferences, but basically what it is is very simply sitting down with a scout and having a discussion with them about scouting and about their progress in scouting.

76
00:12:36,400 --> 00:12:44,400
And as we do that, if we ask them some questions, we get them to begin to exercise the internal standard that we want to build

77
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:49,400
By inquiring about their understanding of the ScoutOath and Law.

78
00:12:50,400 --> 00:12:53,400
And the more practical we can be with those questions, the better.

79
00:12:54,400 --> 00:13:02,400
The more we can refer to things that they have participated in and that have happened to them, the better we can build on that internal standard.

80
00:13:03,400 --> 00:13:10,400
Now, as a matter of course, many of us at a Scoutmaster's Conference are also signing off a couple of common requirements to every rank.

81
00:13:10,400 --> 00:13:13,400
One is Scout Spirit and Active Participation

82
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:19,400
And also their understanding of several points of the Scout Law in the ranks up to first class.

83
00:13:20,400 --> 00:13:25,400
In the more senior ranks we're talking about the way they've performed in a position of responsibility.

84
00:13:26,400 --> 00:13:34,400
So the best way to begin to exercise that internal standard, that standard of judgment that we want them to carry through the rest of their lives,

85
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:40,400
is to ask them how they judge themselves against those requirements.

86
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:45,400
Do they think they've met those requirements? Why do they think they've met them?

87
00:13:46,400 --> 00:13:53,400
How does the ScoutOath and Law bear on the conduct that they have exhibited over the past period of time since our last Scoutmaster Conference?

88
00:13:54,400 --> 00:13:58,400
It's a very rich field of inquiry and, handled correctly,

89
00:13:59,400 --> 00:14:01,400
It's a wonderful way to build that internal standard.

90
00:14:01,400 --> 00:14:06,400
Another really great opportunity for us is when the Patrol Leaders Council is gathered.

91
00:14:07,400 --> 00:14:11,400
Our role as Scoutmasters at the Patrol Leaders Council is principally listening,

92
00:14:12,400 --> 00:14:22,400
And when you listen what you'll hear is you'll hear Scouts exercising that internal standard that we're building according to the ScoutOath and Law.

93
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:28,400
You'll hear them solving problems and making plans and commenting on the way things are going,

94
00:14:28,400 --> 00:14:40,400
And occasionally the opportunity will be presented for you to ask a great question that calls on that internal standard, their understanding of the ScoutOath and Law.

95
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:52,400
You're not offering your understanding of it, quite so much as calling on them to make their own judgments according to their understanding of the ScoutOath and Law.

96
00:14:53,400 --> 00:14:58,400
If they ask you what you think, well, then you have the opportunity to share that.

97
00:14:59,400 --> 00:15:11,400
But instead of making statements or making judgments, if we ask questions that probe their understanding, we're really going to exercise that internal standard.

98
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:18,400
Now we do have an opportunity to make statements and to define that standard in the Scoutmaster Minute

99
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:27,400
And so paying attention to that and making sure that it's a meaningful and understandable and brief, because you get a minute and a minute is 60 seconds, it's not 90 seconds.

100
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:32,400
You know what I say. you've got their attention for so much time, so make it count.

101
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:36,400
But you can talk about that internal standard,

102
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:41,400
You can talk about stories that demonstrate it and build on those principles.

103
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:45,400
So, just to review this concept and this way of looking at things,

104
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:56,400
Our aim is to build character, and character comes from internalizing the ideals and values that are expressed in the ScoutOath and Law,

105
00:15:57,400 --> 00:16:00,400
And each one of us needs to internalize that individually

106
00:16:01,400 --> 00:16:05,400
And there are several ways for Scoutmasters to exercise that internal standard,

107
00:16:06,400 --> 00:16:12,400
As I said in Scoutmasters conferences, at the Patrol Leaders Council meeting and during Scoutmaster Minutes,

108
00:16:12,400 --> 00:16:20,400
In a way that bring these things to the surface and provide Scouts with the opportunity to exercise their understanding of these things.

