00:18:26,280 --> 00:18:32,400
scout helps guide a patrol of younger scouts. And as I remember, this position

125
00:18:32,400 --> 00:18:39,680
of responsibility was established about 20 years ago as part of the first class in

126
00:18:39,680 --> 00:18:44,280
the first year initiative And the logic behind that initiative was that a leading

127
00:18:44,280 --> 00:18:49,280
indicator in membership retention is reaching first class within the first

128
00:18:49,280 --> 00:18:54,000
year. So it was suggested to scouts that this was a really good idea and it

129
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:59,240
could be done by keeping scouts together in a patrol of scouts basically their

130
00:18:59,280 --> 00:19:04,520
own age. And this began with what was called the new scout patrol And this was a

131
00:19:04,520 --> 00:19:09,240
group of scouts bridging over from Weeblos- And if you're not familiar with

132
00:19:09,240 --> 00:19:15,400
Weeblos, that is the oldest division of Cub Scouts- And the tradition in the

133
00:19:15,400 --> 00:19:21,240
BSA is that once they've completed the two years of Weeblos they then join

134
00:19:21,240 --> 00:19:25,000
a scout troop And it was the habit at the time and it remains the habit of a

135
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:29,080
lot of scout troops to take those younger boys and divide them up into

136
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:33,760
existing patrols of older scouts. And research showed 20 years ago that that

137
00:19:33,760 --> 00:19:37,640
wasn't working out very well because it resulted in the attrition of a lot of

138
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:41,920
those younger scouts And it was more promising to keep them together in

139
00:19:41,920 --> 00:19:46,840
their same age group. And to address the concern that a young patrol would

140
00:19:46,840 --> 00:19:52,080
have a lot of difficulty getting by, the position of troop guide was

141
00:19:52,080 --> 00:19:56,000
created, an older, experienced scout who was not functioning as the patrol

142
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,960
leader but as a guide for the younger scouts who elected their own

143
00:19:58,960 --> 00:20:02,320
patrol leader and had their own patrol. Now I don't think this was

144
00:20:02,320 --> 00:20:06,800
strictly a countermeasure for adults monkeying around with patrols, but as

145
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:11,240
an encouragement to create conditions that made it more likely to retain

146
00:20:11,240 --> 00:20:14,760
members by providing a better experience for younger scouts. Some

147
00:20:14,760 --> 00:20:18,760
scouts seem to think that this works very well and they use this kind

148
00:20:18,760 --> 00:20:23,080
of new patrol concept today And some scouts use some kind of

149
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:27,080
adaptation of it. My standard advice that determining who is in what

150
00:20:27,080 --> 00:20:30,920
patrol is up to the Patrol Leaders Council And they do that

151
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:34,120
according to the wishes of the scouts themselves And those

152
00:20:34,120 --> 00:20:38,160
wishes are most often going to be based on ages and friendships.

153
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:41,080
Now I can understand why any scout master would think they

154
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:46,480
could do a better job of setting up patrols and mixing up the ages

155
00:20:46,520 --> 00:20:50,000
and, you know, kind of balancing things out, But I think that's a

156
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:55,520
misguided approach. Boys become scouts for one principal reason,

157
00:20:55,880 --> 00:20:59,720
And that is to go camping and do fun stuff with their friends.

158
00:21:00,480 --> 00:21:03,960
Anything else that happens is great, But from a boy's point of

159
00:21:03,960 --> 00:21:12,200
view, they are scouts for that one reason. In my experience,

160
00:21:12,360 --> 00:21:16,440
you don't increase the efficacy of training younger scouts by

161
00:21:16,440 --> 00:21:20,120
assigning patrols in the way that you think it's going to work

162
00:21:20,120 --> 00:21:24,800
best, by mixing up the ages and moving scouts away from their

163
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:28,680
friends. This really ends up creating more problems than it

164
00:21:28,680 --> 00:21:33,360
solves. I've tried a lots of different ideas about the

165
00:21:33,360 --> 00:21:37,320
composition of patrols And many years ago tried assigning

166
00:21:37,320 --> 00:21:41,040
scouts to patrols by my own best lights And it never really

167
00:21:41,040 --> 00:21:46,840
worked. Friendships and peer groups are much stronger than any

168
00:21:46,840 --> 00:21:50,080
assignment that I can make, And what I found was within the

169
00:21:50,080 --> 00:21:53,520
first few minutes of a troop meeting or a camping trip,

170
00:21:54,080 --> 00:21:58,520
scouts disregarded my patrol assignments to be with their

171
00:21:58,520 --> 00:22:02,200
friends or with their peer group. It was, you know. you

172
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:05,960
would look up and maybe they lined up and started the

173
00:22:05,960 --> 00:22:09,440
meeting with their patrol And that was fine. But as soon as

174
00:22:09,440 --> 00:22:12,440
the meeting got going, they all kind of gravitated towards

